  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 221 PM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nA quiet day has prevailed across the area thanks to the influences \nof building mid-level ridging and lingering surface high pressure. \nWith the 850mb thermal ridge remaining displaced just to the west, \ntemperatures should largely  be similar / within a couple degrees of \nyesterday in most cases. The greatest exception likely being NE \nareas of the CWA and state where temps today will be more on the \norder of 10 degrees warmer, but pleasant in the lwo-mid 70s, as \nsunshine prevails in place of the fully overcast day Sunday. \n\nThe quiet / dry conditions will be pretty short lived though as the \nwell advertised shift to wet conditions takes place through the rest \nof the week. The large scale synoptic pattern remains well \ncorroborated among the deterministic globals and their ensembles. \nThis being nor'easter remnants being ushered eastward, western CONUS \ntrough/cut-off low digging into SW US, and remnants of Hurricane \nPolo beginning to lift northward across Baja California early this \nweek. With the building and eastward shifting mid-level ridging over \nthe central CONUS, deep Gulf moisture too will begin to stream \nnorthward. All of which will prime the wider region for widespread \nshowers and storms and the potential for both river and areal \nflooding. This is further reflected via deterministic PWats of >2.00 \ninches, which would be near or at record levels for this time of the \nyear, based on TOP sounding climatology and at 99th percentile or \nMAX within GEFS/Euro Ensemble SA Tables by later Tuesday onward. On \nthe rainfall efficiency front, the deep influx of moisture \nunsurprisingly aids in pushing warm cloud layer depths towards 4km, \nhinting at potential for efficient rainfall via tropical/warm cloud \nprocesses. Some limitation on rainfall rates though with likely \nweaker instability due to the abundantly ongoing moist profiles, \nwhich may shift the flooding potential away from typical warm season \nflash flooding and toward slower onset areal and river flooding. \nAnyways... \n\nInitial precipitation chances move into the NW CWA later Tuesday as \na surface to low level boundary drifts southward out of the Northern \nPlains. Nocturnal LLJ enhances chances through the over night, and \nthe boundary is expected to hang up over the Nebraska/Iowa areas \nthrough Wednesday as cyclogenesis over the Southern High Plains \nincreases low level flow. This places the greatest initial heavy \nrain and flood potential toward far NE Kansas and NW Missouri and up \nthrough large portions of eastern Nebraska and western to central \nIowa through Wednesday. The NW CWA may just miss out on the heaviest \nrainfall axis, and this uncertainty is reflected in 48hr NBM \n25th/75th ranges of around 1.2 to 3.5 inches through Wednesday \nnight. And keep in mind, NBM will not capture the\npotential/likely embedded higher amounts. Given the potential, \nhave coordinated an initial Flood Watch for said areas with \nnorthern neighbors, beginning Tuesday night and through Thursday\nnight. \n\nAs the synoptic pattern evolves through mid-week, western \ntrough/Polo remnants lifting into the Plains, heavy rain location \nbegins to shift as well; along and ahead of the developing surface \ncold front and further north along the deformation axis by Wednesday \nnight and Thursday. This transition is interesting in that it \"may\" \nresult in portions of the area, including the Kansas Cit Metro, \nbeing gaped or surrounded by the heavier rainfall amounts. Of \ncourse, this too remains a couple/few days out and subtle shifts in \ntracks too will shift the heavy rain axis locations. Regardless, it \nwill rain area wide. Just the nuance of where the heavy rain risks \nshift to after Wednesday. The 24hr NBM 25th/75th ranges are \nunsurprisingly wide (in amount and over a very wide area), from \naround 0.70 to 3.00 inches. Same NBM caveat as above. Generally\nanticipate some expansion to the Flood Watch location over the \nnext couple forecast cycles as details continue to come into \nincreasing focus. Best odds may be over northern areas, where \ndeformation area may linger and soil moistures are most \nsaturated over the area. \n\nArea looks to dry out later Friday and into the weekend, but both \ndeterministic and ensemble guidance hint at some low-end \nopportunities for precipitation to linger over central Missouri \nbefore more substantial drying (cold front) drops in from the north \nSunday.",
